        "text": "14"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Triangle conditions",
        "body": "Let the parallelogram have sides of $a$ and $b$ matchsticks and the longer diagonal $c$ matchsticks. Then $2a+2b+c=42$, so $c$ is even and $a+b=21-\\frac c2$. The diagonal and two sides form a triangle, so $c<a+b$. Since the diagonal is the longer one, the angle opposite it is obtuse, so $c^2>a^2+b^2$.\n\n- $c=14$: $a+b=14$, not greater than $c$. No.\n- $c=12$: $a+b=15$ and $a^2+b^2<144$: $(a,b)=(7,8),(6,9),(5,10),(4,11)$ work ($113,117,125,137$); $(3,12)$ gives $153$, too big. 4 ways.\n- $c=10$: $a+b=16$, but $a^2+b^2\\ge128>100$. No.\n- $c\\le8$: $a^2+b^2$ is even larger compared with $c^2$. No.\n\nThere are $4$ ways.\n\n**Answer: (A) 4**"
      }
    ],
